Russia lists the conditions under which it may proceed to use nuclear weapons:
- receipt of credible information indicating a ballistic missile attack on Russia or its allies;
- use of nuclear weapons or other weapons of mass destruction by an enemy or its allies;
- any attack on critical and military infrastructure resulting in interruptions to its ability to launch a nuclear response;
- an attack with traditional weapons presenting an existential threat to the state.
http://www.opex360.com/2020/06/03/la-russie-precise-les-conditions-qui-pourraient-la-conduire-a-utiliser-larme-nucleaire/

I sent this email on the 11th June, 2020.

In blind copy were:
- the Newsletter (https://www.newsletter.co.uk/);
- Sharon O'Neill of ITV (https://twitter.com/sharon_utv), the lady who interviewed me after the "attempted murder" on behalf of the Irish News (https://www.irishnews.com/);
- one other journalist.

Thus far I have received only a cursory acknowledgement of receipt from the recipient in copy, no other responses.

To be clear, my concerns are that my 'mother' Baroness Nuala O'Loan and her husband Augustine Declan O'Loan may be in possession of knowledge relating to multiple crimes:

- my attempted murder;
- the death of my paternal grandfather;
- the 'suicide' of my cousin, Brian O'Loan;
- the death of Lord Mountbatten (re. the late Raphael O'Loan, then resident in Sligo);
- the death of Robert Fulton, one year above me at St Louis Grammar School, Ballymena;
- the death of Laura Cahoon, a friend who was killed in a road accident near Ballymena.

I am currently not aware of any organisation or institution willing or competent to address my concerns.

Current events in the UK and Ireland:

- Discontentment in the UK is based mostly on inconsistent governance;
- A general election tomorrow would result in a Labour government;
- Starmer is allowing the Conservatives to absorb the decline in popularity resulting from governing at this time;
- The Conservatives are implementing much of Labour's manifesto;
- Both the EU and UK maintain a stance of ambiguity towards, or outright support for, the CCP;
- Ireland's aggressive stance, like that of France, is based on faith in EU support, backed by the CCP and investments in China;
- The Irish government enjoys popular support for its aggressive stance, at home and from the left in the UK and US;
- The only fracture in Irish politics is between traditional Catholics and the progressive left - both are politically aligned with the CCP, both seek to deny the democratic vote to leave the EU;
- The UK appears to be involved in electoral fraud in the US presidential election, in order to impose a Biden administration that would encourage denial of the vote to leave the EU;
- Ireland has been silent on the parallels between Democrat Party electoral fraud and electoral fraud in Ireland and Northern Ireland;
- Northern Ireland has been almost completely excluded from any attempt to leave the EU;
- UK and Irish media have been almost silent on the EU-CCP trade deal, whose timing has been harmonised with the EU-UK trade deal negotiations.
